cellPad: PAD_KEYBOARD_LAYOUT=fps, a shooter's keyboard and mouse (mouse look on macOS).

The pad-shaped keyboard layout cannot aim in Resistance 3: keys hold the right stick at full deflection, so the view
only turns at the game's top rate. The game initialises cellMouse but never reads it (no cellMouseGetData import), so
the mouse has to come in through the pad.

With PAD_KEYBOARD_LAYOUT=fps: WASD = left stick, mouse = right stick, left/right/middle button = R1/L1/R3, wheel =
TRIANGLE, Space = CROSS, C/Ctrl/Backspace = CIRCLE, E/R = SQUARE, Q = TRIANGLE, G/F = L2/R2, Shift = L3, V = R3,
arrows and 1-4 = d-pad, Esc/Enter = START, Tab = SELECT. Each poll the right stick gets the deflection at which the
title turns as fast as the mouse moved since the previous poll: PAD_MOUSE_CURVE_X/_Y give the title's turn rate against
deflection ("deflection:rate,...", the dead zone and curve included), PAD_MOUSE_SENS the rate per point of motion.
The view then turns by a fixed angle per point, up to the title's top stick rate. The macOS mouse
(pad_mouse_macos.m: a local NSEvent monitor, cursor hidden and detached from the mouse while the window is in the
foreground, released on focus loss) is the only host mouse so far; other hosts get the keyboard part (TODO in cellPad.c).

Test: libs/input/tests/test_pad_keyboard_fps.c (curve parsing and inversion, stick bytes, the key and button map,
release on focus loss; build line in the file). test_pad_rumble stubs the new mouse call. Scripted run: walking and
turning in the first level with the layout on.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
